<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/abnormal-cholesterol-levels> a dmo:RiskFactor ;
    rdfs:label "Abnormal Cholesterol Levels" ;
    dmo:evidenceNote "LDL, often called “bad” cholesterol, can build up and clog your blood vessels. High levels of LDL cholesterol raise your risk of developing heart disease. HDL is sometimes called “good cholesterol.” Higher levels of HDL is linked to lower risk for heart disease and stroke. To improve LDL and HDL levels, limit the amount of fat in your eating plan, eat more plant-based foods, and get regular physical activity. Another type of blood fat, triglycerides, also can raise your risk of heart disease when the levels are higher than recommended by your health care team." ;
    dmo:name "Abnormal Cholesterol Levels" ;
    dmo:riskCategory "Modifiable" ;
    dmo:riskFactorCode "abnormal-cholesterol-levels" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "LDL, often called “bad” cholesterol, can build up and clog your blood vessels. High levels of LDL cholesterol raise your risk of developing heart disease. HDL is sometimes called “good cholesterol.” Higher levels of HDL is linked to lower risk for heart disease and stroke. To improve LDL and HDL levels, limit the amount of fat in your eating plan, eat more plant-based foods, and get regular physical activity. Another type of blood fat, triglycerides, also can raise your risk of heart disease when the levels are higher than recommended by your health care team." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/niddk-cardiovascular-disease> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/chronic-kidney-disease> a dmo:RiskFactor ;
    rdfs:label "Chronic Kidney Disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceNote "Heart disease is closely linked with chronic kidney disease, a condition in which your kidneys are damaged and can’t filter blood the way they should. Having diabetes is a risk factor for developing kidney disease, which affects about 40% of people with diabetes. Other risk factors for developing kidney disease are high blood pressure and a family history of kidney failure." ;
    dmo:name "Chronic Kidney Disease" ;
    dmo:riskCategory "NonModifiable" ;
    dmo:riskFactorCode "chronic-kidney-disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Heart disease is closely linked with chronic kidney disease, a condition in which your kidneys are damaged and can’t filter blood the way they should. Having diabetes is a risk factor for developing kidney disease, which affects about 40% of people with diabetes. Other risk factors for developing kidney disease are high blood pressure and a family history of kidney failure." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/niddk-cardiovascular-disease> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/chronic-stress> a dmo:RiskFactor ;
    rdfs:label "Chronic Stress" ;
    dmo:evidenceNote "Long-term stress can raise your blood glucose and blood pressure, but you can learn ways to lower your stress." ;
    dmo:name "Chronic Stress" ;
    dmo:riskCategory "Modifiable" ;
    dmo:riskFactorCode "chronic-stress" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "Long-term stress can raise your blood glucose and blood pressure, but you can learn ways to lower your stress." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/niddk-cardiovascular-disease> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/family-history-of-heart-disease> a dmo:RiskFactor ;
    rdfs:label "Family History of Heart Disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceNote "A family history of heart disease may add to your chances of developing the condition. If one or more of your family members had a heart attack before age 50, you have double the chance of developing heart disease compared with people who have no family history of the disease." ;
    dmo:name "Family History of Heart Disease" ;
    dmo:riskCategory "NonModifiable" ;
    dmo:riskFactorCode "family-history-of-heart-disease" ;
    dmo:evidenceQuote "A family history of heart disease may add to your chances of developing the condition. If one or more of your family members had a heart attack before age 50, you have double the chance of developing heart disease compared with people who have no family history of the disease." ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <https://example.org/dmo/id/guidelineSource/niddk-cardiovascular-disease> .

<https://example.org/dmo/id/riskFactor/high-blood-glucose> a dmo:RiskFactor ;
